item.description:
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Free will is the absence of control, letting go of the need for control, accepting that flowing with the Tao, &lt;strong&gt;one&#039;s chosen path&lt;/strong&gt; is always in harmony.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;The idea of there being a &quot;&lt;strong&gt;one&#039;s chosen path&lt;/strong&gt;&quot; is an example of the illusion of free will. Believing that humans are free to choose anything is a comforting story we tell ourselves. In fact, we are not different from any other animal on earth. Ironically, we only think (imagine) that we are, and relentlessly fabricate narratives to &#039;verify&#039; it. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;On the other hand, if by &quot;free will&quot; you mean that all animals have this capacity, then I fully concur. Of course, that makes the &#039;free&#039; aspect meaningless, which means it all come down to a will-to-live shared by living things.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;However, in my experience cultures view free will as solely a human capacity. We merely think we are different, and dare I say, uniquely so. We really are not any more or less unique than any other animal... meaning each species is unique in its own way. &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I assume this elitist view is driven by our illusion of self (ego). This would not be such a big deal if it weren&#039;t for our &lt;em&gt;disease&lt;/em&gt;, i.e., &lt;a href=&quot;http://centertao.local/tao-te-ching/carl/chapter-71/&quot;&gt;Realizing I don&#8217;t know is better; not knowing this knowing is disease.&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
